d mean the flag ramped from off to fully on within a single minute, 07:10 to 07:11, before the discrete change event at 07:15:51 got logged - suggesting the recorded \"change\" timestamp reflects completion of an internal rollout process rather than the moment the ramp visibly took effect in the evaluation logs.\n\nThe strongest evidence still points to the flag flip: ZeroDivisionError errors line up precisely with the error spike, which is compelling for feature-flag-toggle. But if the mitigation record shows the flag being turned off at 07:19:23 without restoring service, that's a real problem for this hypothesis since we don't have logs afterward to confirm whether it actually failed to recover.\n\nSo maybe there's another explanation - perhaps a concurrent bad deployment shipped buggy code around the same time, and the flag toggle just exposed a latent bug rather than being the root cause itself. I want to widen the change-history window well beyond the last 24 hours to check for any deployment introducing the spend_summary.py bug shortly before the flag flip.\n\n",
